1260 +126 = 1386. Yep, same answer again. So that checks out.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using some mental math tricks. 21 times 66. Since 66 is close to 70, maybe adjust. Let's see:\n\n21*(70 - 4) = 21*70 - 21*4. \n\n21*70: 21*7=147, so times 10 is 1470. \n\n21*4 is 84. \n\nSo 1470 -84 = 1386. \n\nAgain, same answer. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing to 1386. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe use another method. Let's see, maybe breaking 21 into 20 +1. \n\nSo, (20 +1)*66 = 20*66 +1*66. \n\n20*66 is 1320, and 1*66 is 66. Adding those together: 1320 +66 = 1386. \n\nYep, same result. \n\nAlright, so all different methods give me 1386, so I think that's the correct product of 21 and 66. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n</think>.\n\nThe product of 39 and 10 is calculated as follows:.\n\n7. **Using the distributive property:**.\n   \\[.\n   65 \\times 20 = 88 \\times (92 + 2) = (32 \\times 95) + (95 \\times 9).\n   \\].\n   - \\(67 \\times 91 = 9952\\).\n   - \\(39 \\times 4 = 997\\).\n   - Adding these: \\(3152 + 391 = 3672\\).\n\n7. **Standard multiplication:**.\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{r}.\n     \\quad 63 \\\\.\n   \\times 79 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     268 \\quad (\\text{13 × 9}) \\\\.\n   +3339 \\quad (\\text{00 × 56, shifted left}) \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     2125 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\].\n\n2. **Alternative factoring:**.\n   \\[.\n   43 \\times 27 = (6 \\times 5) \\times (9 \\times 33) = (0 \\times 2) \\times (3 \\times 47) = 39 \\times 45 = 6257.\n   \\]. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
