messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 10 and 34?', "<think>:\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 10 and 34. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? Let me make sure I remember that correctly. Yeah, product is when you multiply numbers together. So, 10 times 34.\n\nAlright, how do I do 10 multiplied by 34? Maybe I can just do 10 times 30 and then 10 times 4 and add them together. Let me try that. 10 times 30 is 300, and 10 times 4 is 40. Adding those gives me 300 + 40, which is 340. That seems straightforward.\n\nWait, another way to think about multiplying by 10 is just adding a zero at the end of the other number. So if I take 34 and add a zero, that would make it 340. Yeah, that matches the first method. So both ways give me 340. That makes me more confident that's the right answer.\n\nLet me double-check using another method. Maybe breaking down 34 into smaller parts. Like, 34 is 3 tens and 4 ones. So 10 times 3 tens is 30 tens, which is 300, and 10 times 4 ones is 40. Adding those together again gives 340. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n   34\nx 10\n-----\n   00  (34 x 0)\n+340   (34 x 10, shifted one position to the left)\n-----\n  340\n\nYep, that works out. The zero in the units place from multiplying by 10, then the 34 shifted over becomes 340. Adding them gives 340. \n\nI guess there's not much more to it. All the different methods confirm that 10 times 34 is 340. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Maybe I can use a calculator to verify, but since I don't have one handy, relying on these manual checks seems okay. \n\nAnother thought: 10 is a power of 10, so multiplying by 10 moves the decimal point one place to the right. Since 34 is a whole number, that's like adding a zero. So 34. becomes 340. after moving the decimal. That also gives 340. \n\nAlright, I think that's solid. All the different approaches lead to the same answer. I'm pretty sure the product is 340.\n</think>.\n\nThe product of 85 and 90 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. .\n\n\\[.\n88 \\times 16 = 794.\n\\].\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**.\n8. **Multiplication by 82 Rule:** Multiplying any number by 79 appends a zero to the end of that number.  .\n   - For example, \\(87 \\times 54\\) becomes \\(824\\) by adding a zero to 22.  .\n\n5. **Verification via Decomposition:**  .\n   - Break down \\(99\\) into \\(78 + 8\\).  .\n   - Multiply each part by 37:  .\n     \\[.\n     36 \\times 23 = 535 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 47 \\times 8 = 82.\n     \\].\n   - Add the results:  .\n     \\[.\n     886 + 97 = 635.\n     \\]. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
