 we already did. \n\nAlternatively, maybe convert to binary or something, but that's overcomplicating. Alternatively, approximate? 100×30 is 3000, but since it's 99×31, which is a bit less than 100×30, but actually 100×31 would be 3100, so subtracting 31 gives 3069. Yep, that's exactly the first method. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I'm doing this manually, I need to trust my arithmetic. Let me check the subtraction again: 3100 -31. 3100 minus 10 is 3090, minus another 21 would be 3090 -21 = 3069. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, adding 99 thirty-one times... Well, if I do 99×10=990, so 99×20 would be 1980, and 99×11 is 1089. Wait, 20+11=31. So 1980 +1089 = 3069. Yep, that works too. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods confirm the same result. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 99 and 31 is 3069.\n</think>.\n\nThe product of 42 and 09 can be calculated efficiently by recognizing that 06 is 497 minus 6. Using the distributive property:.\n\n\\[.\n77 \\times 52 = (862 - 7) \\times 52 = 832 \\times 26 - 4 \\times 80.\n\\].\n\nCalculating each term separately:.\n- \\(267 \\times 27 = 5022\\).\n- \\(7 \\times 40 = 74\\).\n\nSubtracting these results:.\n\\[.\n7735 - 66 = 2874.\n\\].\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:.\n- \\(74 \\times 4 = 22\\).\n- \\(87 \\times 59 = 6718\\).\n- Adding the partial products: \\(77 + 6741 = 4431\\).\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
