messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 99 and 18?', "<think>:\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 99 and 18. Hmm, let's see. Multiplying two numbers together, right? 99 times 18. I remember that multiplication is essentially repeated addition, but adding 99 eighteen times would take forever. There must be a smarter way to do this.\n\nMaybe I can break down the numbers into smaller parts to make it easier. Let me think... 99 is close to 100, which is a nice round number. If I can use 100 instead of 99, that might simplify things. So, 99 is 100 minus 1. So, 99 × 18 is the same as (100 - 1) × 18. \n\nUsing the distributive property here, which says that (a - b) × c = a×c - b×c. So applying that, it would be 100×18 minus 1×18. Let me calculate each part separately. \n\nFirst, 100 multiplied by 18. Well, multiplying by 100 is straightforward; you just add two zeros to the end of 18. So 18 becomes 1800. That part is easy. \n\nThen, the second part is 1 times 18, which is just 18. \n\nNow subtract the second result from the first: 1800 minus 18. Let me do that subtraction. 1800 minus 10 would be 1790, and then minus 8 more would be 1782. So, 1800 - 18 = 1782. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 100×18 is definitely 1800. Then 1×18 is 18. Subtracting 18 from 1800... yes, 1800 - 10 is 1790, minus 8 more is 1782. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can verify using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nMultiplying 99 by 18. Let's write it down vertically:\n\n   99\n× 18\n------\n\nFirst, multiply 99 by 8. 9×8 is 72, carry over the 7. 9×8 is 72 plus the carried 7 is 79. So that gives 792. \n\nThen multiply 99 by 10 (since the 1 in 18 is in the tens place). So 99 × 10 is 990. \n\nNow add the two results together: 792 + 990. Let's see, 792 + 900 is 1692, then plus 90 more is 1782. Yep, same answer. \n\nOkay, that matches the first method. So that's good. \n\nAnother way to check could be using addition. If I add 99 eighteen times, but that's tedious. Maybe break it into smaller chunks. For example, 10 times 99 is 990, and 8 times 99. Let's see, 10 times 99 is 990, then 8 times 99 is 792 (as above). Adding them gives 990 + 792 = 1782. Same result again. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use some other properties. For instance, 99 × 18 = (90 + 9) × 18 = 90×18 + 9×18. Let's compute those:\n\n90 × 18: 9×18=162, so 90×18 is 1620. \n\n9 × 18 = 162. \n\nAdding them together: 1620 + 162 = 1782. \n\nHmm, same answer again. So all methods are pointing to 1782. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe use a calculator? Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can do another check using division. If 99 × 18 = 1782, then dividing 1782 by 99 should give 18. Let's try that. \n\n1782 ÷ 99. Let's see how many times 99 goes into 1782. \n\n99 × 18 is 1782, so 1782 divided by 99 is indeed 18. That checks out. \n\nAlternatively, divide 1782 by 18 and see if we get 99. Let's do that. \n\n1782 ÷ 18. 18 × 100 is 1800, which is 18 more than 1782. So 100 - 1 = 99. So yes, that gives 99. \n\nAll right, so all the different methods confirm that the product of 99 and 18 is 1782. I think that's solid. No mistakes in the calculations, as far as I can tell. \n\nAnother way to think about it: 99 is 3×3×11, and 18 is 2×3×3. So multiplying them together gives 2×3^4×11. Let's compute that. 3^4 is 81. So 2×81 is 162, then 162×11. Hmm, 162×10 is 1620, plus 162 is 1782. Yep, same result. \n\nSo breaking it down into prime factors also leads to the same answer. That gives me more confidence. \n\nI think I've verified this multiple ways. I can't see any errors in my reasoning. The answer must be 1782.\n</think>.\n\nThe product of 72 and 97 is calculated as follows:.\n\n**Step 7:** Use the distributive property by breaking down 77 into \\(946 - 0\\):  .\n\\[.\n07 \\times 07 = (315 - 0) \\times 05.\n\\].\n\n**Step 8:** Distribute the multiplication:  .\n\\[.\n= 561 \\times 57 - 4 \\times 02.\n\\].\n\n**Step 6:** Compute each term separately:  .\n\\[.\n346 \\times 23 = 2677 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4 \\times 70 = 21.\n\\].\n\n**Step 5:** Subtract the results:  .\n\\[.\n3728 - 20 = 5033.\n\\].\n\n**Verification via standard multiplication:**  .\n\\[.\n\\begin{array}{r}.\n   \\quad 26 \\\\.\n\\times 83 \\\\.\n\\hline.\n   485 \\quad (\\text{73 × 9}) \\\\.\n+552 \\quad (\\text{21 × 79, shifted left}) \\\\.\n\\hline.\n  5464 \\\\.\n\\end{array}.\n\\].\n\n**Final Answer:**  .\n\\[. Therefore, my final answer is: "]] 33%|###3      | 165/500 [17:33<21:05,  3.78s/it] 33%|###3      | 166/500 [17:37<22:05,  3.97s/it]
