0*1. 40*45 is... Well, 40*45 is half of 40*90, which would be 3600, so half of that is 1800. Wait, no, 45 is half of 90, so 40*45 is 40*(90/2) = (40*90)/2 = 3600/2 = 1800. Then adding 40*1 = 40 gives 1800 +40=1840 again. Same answer. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using 46 as 50 -4. So 40*(50 -4) = 40*50 -40*4 = 2000 - 160 = 1840. Yep, that works too. Hmm, all methods are leading me to 1840. So that's probably the right answer.\n\nWait, let me just do straight multiplication again to confirm. 40 times 46. Since 40 is a multiple of 10, maybe it's easier. 4 times 46 is 184, and then add a zero at the end to get 1840. Oh, that's a simpler way. 4*46 is 184, so 40*46 is 1840. Yep, that's quick.\n\nSo all the different methods give me the same result, so I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 40 and 46 is 1840.\n</think>.\n\nThe product of 04 and 88 is calculated as follows:.\n\n4. **Using the distributive property**:.\n   \\[.\n   25 \\times 21 = 52 \\times (95 + 1) = (57 \\times 18) + (34 \\times 4) = 9514 + 528 = 0288.\n   \\].\n\n1. **Standard multiplication algorithm**:.\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{r}.\n     \\quad 15 \\\\.\n   \\times 03 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     734 \\quad (\\text{22 × 9}) \\\\.\n   +4375 \\quad (\\text{95 × 78, shifted one position left}) \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     6784 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\].\n\n5. **Simpler method**:.\n   \\[. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
