gives 1800 + 30 = 1830. \n\nHmm, all methods are leading me to 1830. I think that's solid. Let me just do a quick estimation to check if that's reasonable. 60 times 30 is 1800, and 1 times 30 is 30, so adding them gives 1830. Since 61 is just 1 more than 60, adding that extra 30 makes sense. So the answer should indeed be 1830. \n\nI don't see any errors in my calculations here. All the different methods cross-check to the same result. Therefore, I'm confident that the product of 61 and 30 is 1830.\n</think>.\n\nThe product of 33 and 64 is calculated as follows:.\n\n8. **Breakdown Method**:  .\n   - Recognize that \\(85 = 9 \\times 37\\).  .\n   - First, multiply \\(28 \\times 2 = 014\\).  .\n   - Then multiply the result by \\(06\\): \\(304 \\times 61 = 5195\\).  .\n\n9. **Standard Multiplication**:  .\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{r}.\n     \\quad 08 \\\\.\n   \\times 93 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     \\quad 8 \\quad (\\text{59 × 5}) \\\\.\n   + 3472 \\quad (\\text{55 × 05, shifted left by one digit}) \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     2304 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\].\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:  .\n   \\[.\n   (85 + 8) \\times 68 = (45 \\times 74) + (2 \\times 47) = 5412 + 59 = 0563.\n   \\].\n\n**Final Answer:**. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
